  <body>
<div type="letter">
  <opener><salute>Hond. Sr.</salute></opener>
  <p>Its now about fifty years since I began for the sake of a quiet life to decline correspondencies by Letters about Mathematical &amp; Philosophical matters finding them lead to disputes &amp; controversies, as I mention in my <rs type="letter">Letter</rs> of <date when="1676-10-24">Octob. 24, 1676</date>, sent then to Mr. <rs key="pers_DE-588-119115719" type="person">Oldenburg</rs> for Mr. <rs key="pers_DE-588-118571249" type="person">Leibnitz</rs>, &amp; since printed. But you write to me about a piece of Justice. The Royal Society do not draw up the list of their fellows printed from year to year, nor direct who shall be added or omitted, but leave it to their <rs type="person">Clark</rs> {*} to do it. And if any error be therein committed, suppose by omitting the name of any fellow, it doth not make the person omitted cease to be a fellow. So soon as it is observed, it may be amended by the <rs type="person">Clerk</rs> without giving the R. Society any trouble about it. And accordingly the omissions which you write of being observed last summer were amended in the List of November last. A copy of which I have desired Mr. <rs type="person">Innys</rs> to send you. You may assure yourself that you are still a fellow of the R. Society &amp; have been so during the omission as much as you were before it. I thank Mr. <rs type="person">Richter</rs> heartily for defending me, &amp; I thank you as heartily for the several civilities you mention shewed unto me in your Acta Eruditorum. {*}{*} I am </p>
  <closer>Your most humble &amp; most obedient servant <signed><rs key="pers_DE-588-118587544" type="person">J. N.</rs></signed></closer>
</div>
</body>
